WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Speaker 1: Well, another day and another major&nbsp;
legislative accomplishment for Joe Biden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.320
whose presidency in the last two months, as I told&nbsp;
you about yesterday and the day before, has really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.560
turned itself around. The Inflation Reduction&nbsp;
Act has now been signed into law by President Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:23.360
Biden. This bill, you might recall, barely passed&nbsp;
the U.S. Senate, 5150. But, sir, how could it add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:29.280
up to 101 votes? Well, it was 5050 with Kamala&nbsp;
Harris as vice president. Is the president of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:36.080
the Senate casting the tie breaking vote, 51. That&nbsp;
was on August 7th. There were no Republican votes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:43.280
We also saw the bill on Friday passed the U.S.&nbsp;
House not particularly widely by a 222 207 margin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.800
And Joe Biden has now signed the bill into law.&nbsp;
And it is indeed major, major legislation. Let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:56.800
take a look at what this bill does. It is a more&nbsp;
than $430 billion package expected to reduce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:06.320
deficit by over $300 billion over ten years.&nbsp;
It's expected to raise $737 billion in revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:12.880
over ten years. And it includes 369 billion in&nbsp;
investment money for climate and energy policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:20.400
$64 billion to extend the Affordable Care Act&nbsp;
policy that reduces health insurance costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.040
To be more honest, sometimes we can maybe more&nbsp;
accurately say is it slows down the rate of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:35.520
increase for health insurance costs. It includes&nbsp;
importantly, notably, a 15% corporate minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.160
tax for companies earning over $1,000,000,000&nbsp;
a year. This is a subject of much discussion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.680
which is for individuals. There has been an&nbsp;
alternative minimum tax for a long time, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:52.560
concept of an alternative minimum tax is you make&nbsp;
$1,000,000 personally. You can use deductions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:58.160
losses and all these different things to offset&nbsp;
the full million in taxes, and you end up owing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.960
zero in order to prevent that. Something called&nbsp;
the Alternative Minimum Tax was established, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.800
says that for a certain level of income, no matter&nbsp;
how many deductions and losses you have to offset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:15.280
taxable income, you have to pay some minimum&nbsp;
tax. We never had that for corporations. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:22.560
bill puts that in place at a 15% rate. It also&nbsp;
puts in place a 1% tax on stock buybacks done by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.040
large corporations. This was something that was&nbsp;
a big deal during the pandemic. You may recall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:36.160
when the Donald Trump coronavirus stimuli&nbsp;
packages were enacted, many corporations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:42.640
from the corporate side of it used the money&nbsp;
they got to just buy back their own stock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:48.800
raising its price. So this bill puts in place a&nbsp;
1% tax when you do stock buybacks. It will reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:55.600
drug prices for Medicare recipients. It will it&nbsp;
expects to collect an additional $124 billion from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:02.080
increased IRS enforcement. So there is money in&nbsp;
the bill controversially. So for many to hire more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.800
IRS agents, the belief is that those additional&nbsp;
agents are going to find tax cheats and recover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.880
124 billion additional dollars. It includes tax&nbsp;
credits to incentivize electric cars and electric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.320
HVAC systems as well as other forms of cleaner&nbsp;
technology. That's great. And it is expected to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:31.120
cut pollution by around 40% below the 25 level by&nbsp;
2030. And it also puts in place a $2,000 a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:38.720
out of pocket cap for Medicare recipients. Now, a&nbsp;
few thoughts on this. Number one, is this a major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:45.600
legislative accomplishment for Joe Biden? Yes, it&nbsp;
is. Will people vote based on this in November?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:52.400
It's not totally clear. We'll see what polling&nbsp;
suggests over the next probably 2 to 3 weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:04:00.080
regardless of your political persuasion. There&nbsp;
are things in this that are beneficial to most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:06.800
of the Americans in the country. For example, the&nbsp;
$2,000 out-of-pocket cap for Medicare recipients.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.480
Medicare recipients are both Democrats and&nbsp;
Republicans. In fact, we it's a more conservative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:17.760
group. We know that every older cohort is slightly&nbsp;
more conservative in the United States right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.160
less pollution as everybody benefits from&nbsp;
less pollution, even if you're against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.960
green energy or whatever. All that. There are&nbsp;
things in here from which everyone benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:34.320
regardless of political persuasion.&nbsp;
Now, I want to take a sort of political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:42.560
overview of the Biden administration generally.&nbsp;
My philosophy is, generally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.640
to judge presidents by what they do more than by&nbsp;
what they don't do. Now, there are exceptions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.400
that. For example, Donald Trump failed to do&nbsp;
many things in terms of his COVID response.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.560
That is an area where you have an acute&nbsp;
situation. And it seems logical to say, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:06.320
all these other world leaders did these things.&nbsp;
Trump failed to do that. That was an acute thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:12.160
But there are people who wrote to me over the last&nbsp;
12 to 24 hours about the Inflation Reduction Act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:18.080
saying, David, this is not good for Biden.&nbsp;
What we need to be focusing on with Biden is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:24.000
he hasn't decriminalize marijuana. He hasn't&nbsp;
forgiven all student debt, by the way. Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.160
did forgive a bunch more student debt overnight.&nbsp;
And we'll talk about that on the bonus show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:36.080
Joe Biden did not do X-Y-Z right. Trump also&nbsp;
didn't decriminalize marijuana. Obama didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.760
decriminalize marijuana. It seems to me if we're&nbsp;
trying to evaluate a president's performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.440
none of them decriminalize marijuana and it sort&nbsp;
of zeros out. I want cannabis decriminalized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:53.200
to be clear, but it seems weird to me to react to&nbsp;
what is clearly a major legislative accomplishment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.960
and to say, Well, Biden hasn't done this other&nbsp;
thing. I tend to judge presidents by the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:01.920
that they do, and this is a very good thing.&nbsp;
This should be screamed from the rooftops on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:08.640
media, social media, etc. by Democrats and&nbsp;
remind people you all benefit from things in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.600
this bill. Even though every Republican in the&nbsp;
Senate voted against it. So are there things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.720
Joe Biden has not done that I would like to see&nbsp;
him do? Absolutely. Have I been critical of Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.160
Biden's lack of accomplishments? I have 100%. But&nbsp;
this is an accomplishment. The jobs numbers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.520
good. The economy is looking very good. Inflation&nbsp;
is coming down. The PACT Act has been signed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.480
for people that think this type of thing is&nbsp;
good. He has gone and he approved the strike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.800
that killed al Qaeda terrorist Ayman al-Zawahiri.&nbsp;
Many of you have concerns about due process. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.880
share those concerns. These are the things he has&nbsp;
done. And it makes absolute and perfect sense for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.840
to give him credit for those and then compare to&nbsp;
the alternative when it comes time. If he runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.760
for reelection, what would this other person who&nbsp;
is eventually going to be the Republican nominee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.080
what would they have done in this situation? They&nbsp;
wouldn't have passed the Inflation Reduction Act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.680
They likely would not have passed the PACT&nbsp;
Act. They wouldn't have done a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.440
things Joe Biden did that are good. And&nbsp;
then we evaluate, well, is it worthy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.600
reelection? That's a question for them down the&nbsp;
road. We don't even know if Biden is running.

